  "first_described": "2015-04",
  "description": "APT30 is a Chinese government-suspected cyber espionage group first documented by FireEye in April 2015, with tooling development dating back to at least 2005. \nThe group targets government and commercial entities in Southeast Asia and India, with a particular focus on ASEAN member states, territorial dispute stakeholders, and journalists covering China-related issues. Its intelligence collection mission aligns closely with Chinese government interests in the SEA region. (FireEye, 2015)\nAPT30 is notable for maintaining and developing the BACKSPACE and NETEAGLE backdoors for over a decade, and for building air-gap crossing capabilities (SHIPSHAPE, SPACESHIP, FLASHFLOOD) into its toolkit from the very beginning of development in 2005. FireEye's analysis of the BACKSPACE controller GUI (named \"NetEagle Remote Control System\" / 网络神鹰远程控制系统) revealed evidence of shift-work operations, target prioritization, and version-controlled malware development, suggesting a well-organized professional team. \nPT Security noted in 2024 that the group remains active with modernized versions of its classic tools plus new backdoors.\nAPT30 is frequently conflated with Naikon (MITRE G0019), a related but distinct activity cluster attributed to PLA Unit 78020. MITRE maintains them as separate entries — G0013 for APT30 and G0019 for Naikon — noting they \"share some characteristics\" but \"do not appear to be exact matches.\"\n",
